prob("C0010", "DGFF over an ergodic uniformly elliptic random conductance model: level sets and extremal process", B, "Problem 16.10, p. 224",
     "For uniformly elliptic conductances with a shift-invariant ergodic law, prove for almost every realisation the analogues of Theorems 2.7 and 9.3 for the associated DGFF.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved",
     note="Bounded search 2026-09-07 recalls a 2021-2022 result of Schweiger and Zeitouni on the maximum of the DGFF in random environment (random conductances) in d=2; whether the full extremal process and the intermediate level sets were also treated was not confirmed. Problem kept for the parts beyond the maximum.")
att("C0010", "Homogenisation of the Green function at all scales plus the DGFF argument.",
    "The quenched Green function of the random conductance model in d=2 is g_eff log|x-y| + O(1) up to the scale where homogenisation holds; the concentric decomposition needs the O(1) error to be controlled uniformly over all dyadic scales near a point, which is a quenched local limit theorem at every scale. This is available for uniformly elliptic ergodic conductances by Andres-Deuschel-Slowik type estimates for the heat kernel, so the intermediate level sets (which only need two-point covariances) follow the DGFF proof with g replaced by g_eff. The extremal-process cluster law, however, depends on the local geometry of the conductances around the maximiser and is therefore itself random (not a constant law), which changes the STATEMENT of the Theorem 9.3 analogue: the cluster law becomes a random law indexed by the environment at the maximiser. This suggests the problem as posed needs reformulation for the extremal process.",
    obstruction="The analogue of the cluster law is environment-dependent; the statement of Theorem 9.3 must be modified before it can be proved.")

prob("C0011", "Scaling limit of local-time level sets of planar random walk in the full range of eta", B, "Problem 16.11, p. 224",
     "Show that a suitably normalised level set (16.19) of the local time of simple random walk on D_N (run to a multiple theta of the cover time), or the associated point measure, admits a scaling limit for all 0 < eta < 1 + (1/2) sqrt theta.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved",
     note="The acquired revision of the notes says an update resolves a related range (Abe's work on trees; a recent posting on the lattice). The exact range covered was not verified in a bounded search; the problem is kept with that caveat.")
att("C0011", "Second Ray-Knight isomorphism to transfer the DGFF level-set result to local times.",
    "By the second Ray-Knight theorem the local time at level theta of the walk is, in law, (1/2)(h + sqrt(2 theta ...))^2 for an independent DGFF-type field h; level sets of the local time at height eta times the cover-time scale correspond to DGFF level sets at height lambda log N with lambda determined by eta and theta, giving eta < 1 + (1/2) sqrt(theta)... in the notation of the notes exactly the range where the corresponding DGFF lambda is below 1. The transfer is rigorous for the part of the level set where the sign of h + constant is positive; the difficulty is the negative branch, where the square masks the sign and the point measures of the two branches are dependent. For eta near the upper endpoint the negative branch contributes a lower-order but not negligible set; separating it needs a joint limit of the two branches, which is again Problem 16.1 in disguise.",
    obstruction="Sign ambiguity in the isomorphism couples two DGFF level sets; no joint limit is available.")

prob("C0012", "Subdiffusive upper bound for the Liouville random walk on the DGFF", B, "Problem 16.12, p. 225",
     "For the random walk X on Z^2 driven by the DGFF with parameter beta > 0 (13.9), show that for each delta > 0, P^0_h(|X_T| <= T^{1/psi(beta)} e^{(log T)^{1/2+delta}}) -> 1 in P-probability.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0012", "Exit-time lower bound from effective resistance: compare the exit time from B(N) with the commute time.",
    "The commute-time identity gives E^0_h tau_{B(N)^c} >= c R_eff(0, B(N)^c) pi(B(N)) where pi is the reversible measure (volume of the Liouville measure of the box). The volume is N^{psi... } of order N^{2 + 2 beta^2 g ...} in the notation of the notes, i.e. N^{psi(beta)} up to N^{o(1)} by the LQG volume estimates, and R_eff(0, B(N)^c) >= (log N)^{1/2 - o(1)} ... the resistance lower bound available is only polylogarithmic. Hence E tau >= N^{psi(beta)} times a polylog, which is the right exponent, but the problem asks for a bound on tau in probability, not in expectation, with a (log T)^{1/2+delta} correction. Converting expectation to probability needs concentration of the exit time, and the exit time is not concentrated at fixed environment because the walk can be trapped near the maximum: the trapping time at the deepest well has a heavy tail. The exponent statement (T^{1/psi(beta)+o(1)}) follows from the expectation bound plus Markov's inequality in one direction; the precise correction is out of reach with the resistance route.",
    key_identity="E tau_B(N) >= c R_eff(0,B(N)^c) mu_h(B(N)) with mu_h(B(N)) = N^{psi(beta)+o(1)}.",
    obstruction="Exit-time concentration fails at fixed environment because of trapping at deep wells; the polylog correction is a trapping statement, not a resistance statement.")

prob("C0013", "Existence of the Liouville Langevin motion as a joint scaling limit", B, "Problem 16.13, p. 226",
     "Under a suitable time change, prove that the pair (h_eps, X_eps) of the regularised field and the associated Langevin-type walk converges jointly in law as eps -> 0 to (h, X) with h a CGFF and X a process with continuous non-constant paths correlated with h.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0013", "Tightness via a Dirichlet-form argument and identification through conformal covariance.",
    "The discrete process is a reversible diffusion with respect to the Liouville measure of the regularised field, with Dirichlet form (1/2) integral |grad f|^2 dx (the Dirichlet form is field-independent, only the speed measure depends on the field). Mosco convergence of the speed measures (the Liouville measures converge) gives convergence of the time-changed Brownian motion, i.e. the marginal law of X converges to Liouville Brownian motion; the joint law with the field converges by the same construction because the time change is a measurable functional of the field. But the process in Problem 16.13 is not the time-changed Brownian motion: it has a drift beta grad h_eps (Langevin), whose limit as eps -> 0 is a distribution and not a function, so the drift term does not converge as a process; the joint limit must be defined through an Ito-type renormalisation of the drift, as for the Liouville Brownian motion with drift. I could not find a formulation of the renormalised drift that is closable in eps; the Dirichlet-form route only gives the drift-free limit.",
    obstruction="The Langevin drift beta grad h_eps diverges; the problem needs a renormalisation scheme which the notes do not specify.")

prob("C0014", "Limit of the normalised log-effective resistance of the DGFF electric network", B, "Problem 16.14, p. 227",
     "For admissible approximations D_N of a domain D and the network with resistances (13.13) built from the DGFF, characterise the limit distribution of x -> (log N)^{-1/2} log R_eff(floor(xN), D_N^c) as N -> infinity.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0014", "Bound log R_eff by the field along an annulus decomposition; compare upper and lower bounds.",
    "Rayleigh monotonicity gives R_eff(x, D_N^c) <= sum over dyadic annuli around x of the resistance across the annulus, each bounded by the maximum of e^{beta h} over the annulus times a constant, so log R_eff <= beta max_{annuli} (h max over annulus) summed... more precisely log R_eff <= log sum_k e^{beta M_k} where M_k is the field maximum over annulus k; by the concentric decomposition M_k is the walk value at scale k plus a bounded extremal term, so log R_eff is bounded above by beta times the maximum over k of the concentric walk, which after dividing by (log N)^{1/2} converges to beta sqrt g times the maximum of a Brownian motion on [0,1]. The lower bound via cutsets gives the same functional with the minimum over annuli of the field minimum, i.e. a different functional. The two bounds do not match: the upper bound is a maximum of a Brownian motion and the lower bound is a minimum, so the limit distribution is sandwiched between beta sqrt g times max and min of a Brownian motion but not identified. The notes' guess of a CGFF limit for the field x -> log R_eff is consistent with this sandwich only if the resistance is dominated by the coarse scale.",
    key_identity="beta sqrt g min_{[0,1]} B <= liminf (log N)^{-1/2} log R_eff <= beta sqrt g max_{[0,1]} B in distribution (heuristic sandwich).",
    obstruction="Upper (flow) and lower (cutset) bounds converge to different functionals of the concentric walk.")

prob("C0015", "Scaling limit of the current-minimising path in the DGFF network", B, "Problem 16.15, p. 228",
     "Prove that the joint law of the DGFF and the random path P minimising the effective-resistance energy between two boundary points admits a non-degenerate scaling limit.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0015", "Tightness of the path via the exponential metric (Liouville first passage percolation) comparison.",
    "The energy-minimising path between two points concentrates on paths of small total resistance, i.e. on near-geodesics of the discrete metric with edge weights e^{beta h}; this is the Liouville first passage percolation metric, whose scaling limit (the LQG metric) exists for beta below the critical value by Ding-Dubedat-Dunlap-Falconet and Gwynne-Miller. Tightness of P is then plausible via geodesic tightness in that metric, but P is not a geodesic: the current spreads over many paths and the minimiser of the quadratic energy is a flow, not a path; the notes' 'path' is the sampled path of the current. Its scaling limit would be a random path sampled from the limiting current, which requires the continuum current on LQG, an object not yet constructed. I record the reduction: existence of the limit of P is equivalent to existence of the continuum electrical current between boundary points on the LQG surface.",
    obstruction="No continuum electrical current on LQG has been constructed; the path is a functional of it.")

prob("C0016", "Hausdorff dimension of the continuum DGFF current path and of the energy support", B, "Problem 16.16, p. 228",
     "What is the Hausdorff dimension of the continuum random path of Problem 16.15, and, for the electric current between opposite sides of a square, what is the dimension of the support of the energy?",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0016", "Compare with known dimensions of LQG geodesics and of the LQG metric ball boundary.",
    "If the continuum path were an LQG geodesic its Euclidean Hausdorff dimension would be that of LQG geodesics, which is strictly greater than 1 and below 2 and only known within bounds for general gamma; the energy support, if concentrated on a fractal set, would have dimension between the geodesic dimension and 2. I could not do better than these bounds because the object (C0015) is not constructed. A rigorous discrete statement is available: the discrete energy of the minimising current between opposite sides of [0,N]^2 is bounded above and below by constants times the effective conductance, whose growth exponent is the same as that of the resistance in C0014, so the energy is not carried by a set of full dimension 2 whenever the resistance exponent is non-trivial.",
    obstruction="Continuum object not defined; only the discrete energy scaling is accessible.")

