prob("C0020", "Optimal exponent for the computational complexity of randomised algorithms for percolation crossings", G, "Chapter VIII Section 4, p. 92",
     "Find the smallest sigma such that there are randomised algorithms A_n deciding the left-right crossing f_n of an n x n box in critical percolation with computational complexity C(A_n) <= n^sigma; known sigma in [6/4, 7/4].",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0020", "Lower-bound the complexity by the OSSS inequality with the sharp four-arm exponent and compare with the exploration-path algorithm.",
    "The exploration algorithm reveals the pivotal-rich interface, of expected size n^{7/4+o(1)}, giving sigma <= 7/4. The lower bound 6/4 in the notes comes from Theorem VIII.6 (revealment times complexity). A better lower bound would follow from the OSSS inequality Var f <= sum_i delta_i I_i if one could bound the revealments delta_i by C(A)/n^2 uniformly, giving C(A) >= n^2 Var f / (sum_i I_i) = n^2 / (n^2 alpha_4(n)) = n^{5/4}, which is WEAKER than 6/4. So OSSS with the 4-arm exponent cannot beat 6/4; the 6/4 bound uses the stronger Theorem VIII.6. Improving the lower bound needs a new inequality between complexity and the spectral measure beyond the second Fourier level; I checked that the third-level analogue of OSSS (via the pivotal-triples) gives at best n^{6/4} again because three-pivotal points have density n^2 alpha_6... which is smaller. No improvement.",
    obstruction="Both OSSS-type bounds saturate at 6/4; closing the gap to 7/4 needs a genuinely new lower-bound technique.")

prob("C0021", "Revealment exponent of the iterated 3-majority function", G, "Exercise VIII.5 note, p. 97",
     "The revealments of the iterated 3-majority function on 3^k bits decay like n^{-sigma}; determine sigma.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0021", "Compute the revealment of the natural recursive algorithm exactly and bound the optimal one from below via Theorem VIII.7.",
    "The recursive algorithm (evaluate two random children, evaluate the third only if they disagree) reveals a given leaf with probability (2/3 + (1/3) p_disagree)^k where at each level the probability the third child is needed is 1/2 (two independent uniform bits disagree with probability 1/2), so delta = (5/6)^k = n^{-log(6/5)/log 3} = n^{-0.166}. Lower bound: Theorem VIII.7 gives delta >= Var(f)/(n max_i I_i) with I_i = (3/2)^k / 3^k ... the influence of a leaf in iterated 3-majority is (3/2)^k/3^k... wait each level multiplies influence by 3/2 relative to 1/3 branching: I_i = (1/2)^k ... corrected: I_i = (3/4)^k? Direct recursion: the influence of a leaf is prod over levels of P(the other two children disagree)=1/2, so I_i = 2^{-k} = n^{-log 2/log 3} = n^{-0.631}; then delta >= 1/(n n^{-0.631}) = n^{-0.369}. So sigma in [0.166, 0.369] for the exponent of the optimal revealment. The gap is between the greedy recursive algorithm and the Theorem VIII.7 lower bound; I could not find an algorithm beating (5/6)^k nor a better lower bound.",
    key_identity="Greedy recursive algorithm: delta = (5/6)^k; leaf influence 2^{-k}; hence log(6/5)/log 3 <= sigma <= 1 - log 2/log 3.",
    check="k=1: greedy reveals a given leaf with probability 2/3 + 1/3 * 1/2 = 5/6, matches; influence of a leaf in majority of 3 = 1/2, matches.",
    obstruction="No matching algorithm or lower bound; the true sigma lies in [0.166, 0.369].")

prob("C0022", "Singularity of the scaling limits of the spectral sample and of the pivotal set", G, "Chapter X Section 2, p. 111",
     "Prove that the scaling limits of (1/n) S_{f_n} (spectral sample) and (1/n) P_{f_n} (pivotal set) of the crossing event are singular random compact sets, and in particular that the spectral sample has a scaling limit.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved",
     note="Garban-Pete-Schramm (2013) constructed the scaling limit of the pivotal MEASURE; the notes' statement is about the sets and about the spectral sample. Not verified further.")
att("C0022", "Compare the two-point functions of the two random sets.",
    "The pivotal set and the spectral sample have the same one-point intensity (alpha_4 at each point) and the same two-point function up to constants (both are asymp alpha_4(|x-y|) alpha_4(|x-y|, n) ...), so any singularity must come from higher-order structure. The known difference is in the third moment: E|S|^3 and E|P|^3 differ, and more precisely the conditional law of S given a point x in S has a strictly different clustering structure (spectral sample is 'more clustered'). A proof of mutual singularity of the limiting sets would follow from a law-of-large-numbers statistic that separates them, e.g. the number of points at distance < eps of a typical point normalised by eps^{-3/4}; the limits of these normalised counts are random variables whose laws differ, but singularity needs an a.s. separating statistic, not one in law. Reduction recorded, no proof.",
    obstruction="One needs an almost-sure local statistic separating the two sets; only distributional differences are known.")

prob("C0023", "No percolation at criticality on Z^d for 3 <= d <= 10", G, "Chapter XI Section 1, p. 136; also duminil_copin_icm_2018 Conjecture 1 p. 3 and duminil_copin_ising_potts_2017 Conjecture 1 p. 37",
     "Prove theta(p_c) = 0 for Bernoulli percolation on Z^d for every d >= 3; open for 3 <= d <= 10 (known for d >= 11 by Fitzner and van der Hofstad, and for d = 2).",
     "source_says_open", "no_resolution_found_in_documented_search", "attempted_unsolved",
     dedup="theta_pc_zero_intermediate_dimensions",
     note="Bounded search 2026-09-07: no resolution for 3 <= d <= 10 found; the d >= 11 result (Fitzner-van der Hofstad 2017) and the half-space result of Barsky-Grimmett-Newman remain the state of the art.")
att("C0023", "Try to bootstrap the half-space result of Barsky-Grimmett-Newman to the full space via a finite-energy and uniqueness argument.",
    "In the half-space N x Z^{d-1} at p_c(Z^d) there is no infinite cluster (BGN). If the full space had an infinite cluster C at p_c, it would be unique (Burton-Keane), and by ergodicity and finite energy it would intersect every half-space; the half-space restriction of C is not the half-space percolation cluster (edges crossing the boundary are removed), and the half-space result only says the half-space's OWN clusters are finite, so C restricted to a half-space is a union of infinitely many finite half-space clusters connected through the other side. The natural next step is to show that infinitely many finite half-space clusters cannot connect through a hyperplane without creating an infinite half-space cluster on one side at a slightly larger p, i.e. to use the sharpness of the transition (Duminil-Copin-Tassion) in the half-space: at p_c the half-space cluster of the origin has infinite expected size? Not known: the half-space susceptibility at p_c(Z^d) could be finite, in which case the argument fails. I checked the d=2 analogue where everything is known: there the argument works because the half-plane susceptibility at 1/2 is infinite. So the reduction is: theta(p_c)=0 in Z^d follows if the half-space susceptibility at p_c(Z^d) is infinite together with a gluing lemma; the first statement is open.",
    obstruction="Infinite half-space susceptibility at p_c and a gluing lemma through the hyperplane are both unproved for 3 <= d <= 10.")

