prob("C0029", "Spielman-Teng conjecture on the least singular value of Bernoulli matrices", BC, "Section 7, p. 53",
     "For X with i.i.d. symmetric Bernoulli entries, show P(sqrt n s_n(X) <= t) <= t + c^n for some 0 < c < 1, all large n and all small t >= 0.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved",
     note="Bounded recall 2026-09-07: the t = 0 case, P(s_n = 0) = (1/2 + o(1))^n, was proved by Tikhomirov (Ann. Math. 2020); Rudelson-Vershynin (2008) give C t + c^n with an absolute constant C. Whether C can be taken equal to 1 as conjectured was not found resolved.")
att("C0029", "Test whether the constant 1 in front of t is forced by the Gaussian comparison and whether the Bernoulli case can beat it.",
    "For the Gaussian matrix Edelman's exact law gives P(sqrt n s_n <= t) = 1 - e^{-t^2/2 - t} ~ t as t -> 0, so the constant 1 is exactly the Gaussian density at zero of sqrt n s_n; the conjecture says Bernoulli matrices are no worse than Gaussian up to the exponentially small singular mass. A sufficient condition would be a local limit theorem for sqrt n s_n at zero uniform in n, which is a universality statement at the hard edge (the smallest singular value) and is known (Tao-Vu 2010 hard-edge universality) only in the form P(sqrt n s_n <= t) = (1 - e^{-t^2/2-t}) + O(n^{-c}) for t in a fixed range, which gives the conjecture for t >= n^{-c'} but not for t between c^n and n^{-c'}. The gap regime is exactly the small-ball regime where the Rudelson-Vershynin constant C appears. No improvement obtained.",
    key_identity="Gaussian case: P(sqrt n s_n <= t) = 1 - exp(-t^2/2 - t), so the conjectured constant is the Gaussian value.",
    obstruction="Hard-edge universality covers t >= n^{-c}; the conjecture needs the constant 1 down to exponentially small t.")

prob("C0030", "Circular law and real-root count for Gaussian Weyl polynomials", BC, "Section 7, p. 54",
     "For Weyl polynomials sum_k b_k z^k / sqrt(k!) with i.i.d. standard Gaussian b_k, prove that the empirical distribution of roots tends to the uniform law on a disc and that in the real Gaussian case there are about (2/pi) sqrt n real roots.",
     "source_says_open", "resolved_in_literature", "not_attempted_resolved",
     note="Kabluchko and Zaporozhets ('Asymptotic distribution of complex zeros of random analytic functions', Ann. Probab. 2014) proved the circular-type law for Weyl polynomials; the expected number of real roots (2/pi) sqrt n follows from the Kac-Rice formula and was computed for the Weyl ensemble (Edelman-Kostlan 1995 type computation); universality of the real-root count for non-Gaussian coefficients by Tao-Vu (2015) and Do-Nguyen-Vu. Bounded recall.",
     resolved_by="Kabluchko-Zaporozhets 2014; Edelman-Kostlan; Tao-Vu 2015")

prob("C0031", "Almost-sure convergence in the heavy-tailed circular law", BC, "Section 6.6 remark, p. 39",
     "Upgrade the convergence in probability of the empirical spectral distribution of n^{-1/alpha} X (i.i.d. entries in the domain of attraction of an alpha-stable law, alpha in (0,2)) to almost-sure weak convergence to mu_alpha.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0031", "Borel-Cantelli over the Hermitisation: identify which estimate is only in probability.",
    "The proof of Theorem 6.2 has three inputs: (i) convergence of the singular-value distributions of n^{-1/alpha}X - z for each z, which is almost sure by the objective method (the local weak convergence to the PWIT holds a.s.); (ii) uniform integrability of log at infinity, a.s. from moment bounds; (iii) uniform integrability of log near zero, i.e. a lower bound on the small singular values, which is where only an in-probability bound s_n(n^{-1/alpha}X - z) >= n^{-c} with probability 1 - o(1) is available. To make (iii) almost sure one needs the failure probability summable in n, i.e. a bound like P(s_n <= n^{-c}) <= n^{-1-eps}. For heavy tails the row norms are not concentrated and the Rudelson-Vershynin small-ball argument gives only polynomial failure probabilities with exponent that is not obviously above 1. If the failure probability were shown to be O(n^{-1-eps}) (for instance via an Esseen-type anti-concentration in the alpha-stable domain, which gives exponent depending on alpha), Borel-Cantelli finishes the a.s. statement. So the problem reduces to a quantitative smallest-singular-value bound for heavy-tailed matrices with summable failure probability; the bound is plausible but I did not prove it.",
    obstruction="Need P(s_n(n^{-1/alpha}X - z) <= n^{-c}) summable in n for heavy-tailed entries.")

prob("C0032", "Explicit form of the heavy-tailed circular law density", BC, "Section 6.6, p. 39",
     "Identify explicitly the isotropic density g_alpha(|z|) of the limiting spectral law mu_alpha of n^{-1/alpha}X for alpha-stable-type entries; only g_alpha(0) and the tail (r^{2(alpha-1)} e^{-alpha r^alpha/2} up to constants) are known.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0032", "Attempt to derive g_alpha from the PWIT recursive distributional equation for the resolvent at z, then differentiate in z.",
    "The density is g_alpha(r) = (1/2pi) Delta_z of the log-potential U(z) = -integral log|z-w| dmu_alpha(w), and U is expressed through the limit of the singular-value law of the shifted operator on the PWIT, whose Stieltjes transform satisfies a fixed-point equation in a 2x2 matrix-valued function with alpha-stable-type nonlinearity (as in Bordenave-Caputo-Chafai). The z-dependence enters only through the off-diagonal entries; at z = 0 the equation closes on a single positive stable random variable, which is why g_alpha(0) is explicit. For z not 0 the fixed point involves a two-parameter family of stable laws (radial and angular parts), and I could not reduce it to a scalar equation; the isotropy only removes the angular variable of z, not of the recursion. Tail check: the stated tail r^{2(alpha-1)} e^{-alpha r^alpha/2} is consistent with the expected behaviour that eigenvalues of modulus r come from rows with an exceptionally large entry of size r^alpha, whose density in the stable domain is r^{-alpha-1} ... the exponential factor indicates instead a large-deviation of a sum of alpha-stable variables of the Poisson-Dirichlet type. No closed form.",
    obstruction="The matrix-valued PWIT fixed point does not reduce to a scalar equation away from z = 0.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Lyons, determinantal probability
L = "lyons_determinantal_2014"
prob("C0033", "Concavity of the entropy of determinantal measures in the kernel", L, "Conjecture 2.6, p. 5 and Conjecture 5.3, p. 22",
     "For positive contractions Q_1, Q_2 on l^2(E), E finite, Ent(P_{(Q_1+Q_2)/2}) >= (Ent(P_{Q_1}) + Ent(P_{Q_2}))/2; equivalently (Conjecture 5.3) the entropy H(P_f) of stationary determinantal processes is concave in f.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "partial_result",
     note="Not verified against later literature in this session.")
att("C0033", "Numerical test on random pairs of positive contractions, plus the diagonal and one-element cases in closed form.",
    "Exact facts: for |E| = 1 the measure is Bernoulli(q) and Ent = h(q) is concave, so the conjecture holds; for commuting diagonal kernels the measure is a product and Ent = sum h(q_i), concave; for Q_2 = I - Q_1 the midpoint is I/2, whose entropy |E| log 2 is the maximum possible, so the inequality holds trivially. Numerical check (raw/C_lyons_conj26_entropy_check.json): 1440 random pairs of symmetric positive contractions in dimensions 2..6 with spectra in (0.02, 0.98), entropy computed exactly from P(A) = det(I-Q) det(J_A) with J = Q(I-Q)^{-1}; the concavity gap Ent(mid) - average was positive in every trial, minimum gap 8.5e-4 (dimension 2). No counterexample; the minimum gap being attained in dimension 2 suggests that a proof for 2x2 kernels, where the measure has four atoms with explicit determinants, would be the first non-trivial case to attempt analytically.",
    key_identity="P_Q(A) = det(I-Q) det(J_A), J = Q(I-Q)^{-1}, gives an exact finite formula for the entropy.",
    check="Probabilities summed to 1 within 1e-8 in every trial and were non-negative.",
    obstruction="No analytic argument beyond diagonal kernels; the 2x2 case is the natural next target.")

prob("C0034", "Coupling of determinantal projection measures with union marginal", L, "Question 2.8, p. 6",
     "Given H = H_1 (+) H_2 orthogonal in l^2(E), is there a coupling of P_{H_1} and P_{H_2} whose union has law P_H?",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "partial_result")
att("C0034", "Exact linear-programming feasibility of the coupling on random and structured instances.",
    "Since |X_1| = dim H_1, |X_2| = dim H_2 and |X_1 union X_2| = dim H almost surely, any such coupling has X_1 and X_2 disjoint a.s.; the question is therefore the feasibility of the transportation polytope with marginals P_{H_1}, P_{H_2} and the disjoint-union marginal P_H. I solved this LP exactly (HiGHS, minimum total constraint violation) on 1360 random pairs of orthogonal subspaces of l^2(E) with |E| in {3,...,6} and all dimension splits, on generic instances with |E| = 7, 8, on Fourier-mode subspaces on cycles of length 5..8, and on star-space splits in the edge space of K_4 (uniform spanning tree kernels): every instance was feasible with zero violation (raw/C_lyons_q28_lp_check.json, raw/C_lyons_q28_lp_check_structured.json). This extends the numerical support the source mentions. A structural observation: the feasibility is equivalent to the existence of a Markov kernel from P_H to pairs, i.e. a random rule splitting a P_H-sample C into (C_1, C_2) with the right marginals; for H = l^2(E) the rule is trivial. I looked for a canonical rule (sample X_1 from P_{H_1} conditioned on X_1 subset of C, then X_2 = C minus X_1) and checked on |E| = 4 that this canonical rule does NOT give the correct marginal for X_2 in general, so the coupling, when it exists, is not the naive conditional one.",
    key_identity="Coupling exists iff the transportation polytope {mu >= 0 : marginals P_{H_1}, P_{H_2}, P_H on disjoint unions} is nonempty; nonempty in all 1360+ tested instances.",
    check="LP built from exact determinantal probabilities det(K_A); infeasibility measured as minimum L1 slack, zero to solver precision.",
    obstruction="No general construction; the naive conditional splitting fails.")

prob("C0035", "Determinantal processes with trigonometric-polynomial symbol as block factors", L, "Question 5.4, p. 22",
     "Let f : T -> [0,1] be a trigonometric polynomial of degree m; the stationary determinantal process P_f on Z is m-dependent. Is P_f an (m+1)-block factor of an i.i.d. process? Known for m = 1.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "attempted_unsolved")
att("C0035", "Compare with the structure of m-dependent processes that are not block factors, and test the necessary condition of being a factor through the finitary coding literature.",
    "For m = 1 the known proof writes P_f as a 2-block factor using the fact that a 1-dependent determinantal process on Z with symbol a + b cos(theta) ... has a specific one-dimensional family of conditional laws. For general m, a necessary condition for an (m+1)-block factor is that the process be m-dependent, which holds; the known obstructions to block factors (Aaronson-Gilat-Keane-de Valk examples of 1-dependent non-2-block-factor processes) live in processes whose 3-point marginals cannot be realised by a 2-block factor, which is detected by the sign of certain determinants of the marginal probabilities. For P_f with m = 2, the two-block-factor obstruction of AGKdV does not apply (it concerns 1-dependence), and the 3-block factor question needs a 3-block realisability criterion, which is not available in closed form. I recorded the first candidate test: for f(theta) = 1/2 + (1/2) cos(2 theta) (m = 2, a 2-dependent process that is a Bernoulli(1/2) process with decoupled even and odd sublattices... this specific f gives independent sublattices each determinantal with degree-1 symbol in the sublattice variable, hence a 3-block factor by applying the m = 1 result on each sublattice); this settles the question positively for symbols that are polynomials in cos(2 theta), i.e. f(theta) = g(2 theta) with g of degree m/2, when m is even, by the sublattice decomposition and induction.",
    key_identity="If f(theta) = g(k theta) then P_f is the interleaving of k independent copies of P_g on the sublattices kZ + j, so block-factor status reduces to that of g.",
    check="The kernel entry K(x,y) = f-hat(x-y) vanishes unless k divides x-y, giving independence of the sublattices; the k = 2, m = 2 case reduces to m = 1 which is known.",
    obstruction="General degree-m symbols without a sublattice structure are not covered; no (m+1)-block realisability criterion.")

prob("C0036", "Fuglede-Kadison product-measure bounds for equivariant determinantal processes", L, "Conjecture 5.7, p. 23",
     "For every Gamma-equivariant positive contraction Q on l^2(Gamma), P_Q stochastically dominates the product measure with density FK(Q) = exp tr log|Q| and is dominated by the product measure with density 1 - FK(I-Q), and these bounds are optimal; open even for finite groups.",
     "source_says_open", "unverified", "partial_result")
att("C0036", "Prove a weaker rigorous bound via one-site conditional probabilities and test the conjectured bound exactly on cyclic groups by monotone-coupling linear programs.",
    "Rigorous partial result. For a finite group Gamma and an equivariant Q with 0 < Q < I, P_Q(X = A) = det(I-Q) det(J_A) with J = Q(I-Q)^{-1}, so P(e in X | X minus e = B) = s_B/(1+s_B) with s_B the Schur complement J_ee - J_{eB} J_B^{-1} J_{Be} = 1/((J_{B union e})^{-1})_{ee}, which is decreasing in B; its minimum over B is 1/(J^{-1})_{ee} = 1/((Q^{-1})_{ee} - 1). Equivariance gives (Q^{-1})_{ee} = (1/|Gamma|) sum over characters of 1/lambda_chi = 1/HM where HM is the harmonic mean of the spectrum, so every one-site conditional probability of presence is at least HM. By Holley's inequality specialised to a product measure (its condition reduces to the one-site statement that every conditional probability of presence given the rest is at least p), P_Q dominates the product measure of density HM(Q), and symmetrically is dominated by the product measure of density 1 - HM(I-Q). Since HM <= GM = FK, this is weaker than the conjecture, and the conjecture's content is exactly the improvement from harmonic to geometric mean. Numerical test of the conjecture itself: on Z/nZ, n = 3..6, 102 random symmetric circulant contractions, the monotone-coupling LP (minimum violation) certifies BOTH conjectured dominations with zero violation in every instance (raw/C_lyons_conj57_fk_domination_check.json); optimality check on one n = 5 instance: raising the product density from FK to FK + 0.01 already makes the lower domination infeasible (violation 0.006), so the FK bound is sharp to within 0.01 there.",
    key_identity="min over B of P_Q(e in X | X minus e = B) = 1/(J^{-1})_{ee} = HM(spec Q) for equivariant Q on a finite group; hence P_Q >= Bernoulli(HM(Q)) rigorously, versus the conjectured Bernoulli(GM(Q)).",
    check="Schur-complement monotonicity: (M_S^{-1})_{ee} is nondecreasing in S for positive definite M (Cauchy interlacing / Schur complement); the domination criterion is the classical one-site condition for product measures.",
    obstruction="Closing the gap between harmonic and geometric mean requires using conditional probabilities given partial information, not the worst-case conditioning.")

